@@ -0,0 +1,303 @@
+# Crabcraft skills: six moves for "World of Crabcraft"
+
+"World of Crabcraft" is a browser RPG whose every character is this robot. 31 of its 37
+motion clips are recorded rollouts of this repository's trained policies; six were hand
+keyframes. The six tasks here are those six moves as reference clips a policy can be
+trained to track, so that every move in the game comes from a trained policy and each
+could also run on a physical jumper.
+
+| task | replaces | the move |
+|---|---|---|
+| `jumper.skill_swipe` | `attack_swipe` | the right claw winds up high and sweeps across the front; the left claw stays down |
+| `jumper.skill_slam` | `attack_slam` | rock back, both claws up and open, down onto the floor in front |
+| `jumper.skill_cast` | `cast` | rock back, claws gathered, raised and held through a flourish, flung wide on the release |
+| `jumper.skill_flinch` | `hit_react` | the shell jolts back and nose-up while the left claw snaps up across the visor |
+| `jumper.skill_powerdown` | `death` | the legs give way one at a time and the shell settles on the floor; held; back up |
+| `jumper.skill_sit` | `sit` | the feet step out, the rear sits down on the floor with the front up on the claws; held; back up |
+
+Each task is built the way `jumper.gesture_salute` is: the dance machinery
+(`tasks/jumper/common/dance/env.py`), 50 Hz, all 22 joints, the tracking rewards, with
+every std and threshold recomputed from its own clip in its `env_cfg.py`. The clips are
+committed in each task's `media/` and were written by
+[`tools/author_skill_clips.py`](../tools/author_skill_clips.py), whose docstring says how
+a clip is made and what each check below measures. To write them again:
+
+```bash
+python tools/author_skill_clips.py                     # all six
+python tools/author_skill_clips.py --check --dry-run   # the checks below, physics included; writes nothing
+```
+
+All six are trained (below, "Trained"), exported to `tasks/jumper/skill_*/out/crabcraft`
+and bundled: `deploy/crabcraft.manifests.json` holds the `crabcraft` bundle, the `jumper` bundle
+plus the six skills, each on Menu + a pad button and on a digit key, from locomotion (below,
+"The crabcraft bundle").
+
+## Three things about this robot that shaped every move
+
+**The centre of mass is ahead of the middle feet**: 10 mm ahead of the base origin at
+`HOME`, 18 mm with the claws reaching out from a body moved back, against middle feet
+7 mm behind it. With both claws off the floor the jumper tips onto them. The slam and
+the cast move the body 40 mm back before the claws lift and set them down before it
+comes forward; the swipe and the flinch lift one claw and stand on the other.
+
+**The shell cannot reach the floor from the `HOME` footprint.** Lowered over its feet,
+the knees pass their soft limits 27 mm down and meet their stops at 45 mm, with the shell
+still 25 mm off the floor. The power-down steps each walking foot 40 mm out first, the
+sit its rear feet 40 mm back and out and its middle feet 20 mm out, each foot lifted and
+set down, never slid; both step them back before standing up. The shell rests on its
+servo housings (the claws' upper-arm hubs, the shoulders and the hips), not on the base
+plate, which they hang 24 to 37 mm below.
+
+**Balance, not torque, sets the speed.** Every move scanned reaches the point where its
+base can no longer be balanced on its contacts, with the feet at friction 0.5, while the
+least joint torque it needs is still at most 1.19 N*m, under the servo's 1.2 N*m
+continuous rating. The swipe plays at 0.9 of its keyed times, the
+fastest that balances; the flinch at its keyed times, which already are. Scans below.
+
+## The game's windows
+
+The game plays a clip, or part of one, with its `from` and `to` options in clip seconds.
+The same moments are in each clip as `events` (JSON, seconds), so the export step that
+turns rollouts into game clips can read them rather than copy them from here.
+
+| task | clip | move | window to play | moments inside it |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| swipe | 2.12 s | 0.94 s | 0.00 to 0.94 | wind-up peak 0.44, hit (claw crossing the front) 0.60 |
+| slam | 3.38 s | 1.80 s | 0.40 to 1.50 | claws at the top 0.90, impact (pads on the floor) 1.12 |
+| cast | 4.86 s | 3.40 s | 0.40 to 3.10 | gather 0.68; channel 1.00 to 2.20, the same pose at both ends, loopable; release 2.40 |
+| flinch | 1.38 s | 0.46 s | 0.00 to 0.46 | hit 0.00, recoil peak 0.16 |
+| powerdown | 5.68 s | 4.76 s | 0.00 to 2.05, then hold | collapse from 0.30, shell down 1.60, settled 2.05 to 3.20; rising 3.60 to 4.76 |
+| sit | 5.32 s | 4.40 s | 0.80 to 1.70, then hold | seated 1.70 to 2.90 (one breath, loopable); rising 2.90 to 4.40 |
+
+Every clip starts at `HOME` and ends with the gestures' lead-out back to it and 0.5 s
+standing there, because the controller ramps to `HOME`, plays the recording, and hands
+back to the walking policy. Outside the window is what the robot needs and the game does
+not show.
+
+## Feasibility, per clip
+
+All measured by `tools/author_skill_clips.py --check` on this model (Apple M4 Pro,
+macOS, 2026-10-14). The clip's base pose is the keyed one; "solved" is the same pose
+found again from the joints alone by `import_wbc_gestures.resting_floor`, the control.
+
+### The clip as written
+
+| | swipe | slam | cast | flinch | powerdown | sit |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| base height, mm | 103.5 to 110.5 | 94.5 to 120.5 | 100.5 to 126.5 | 104.5 to 111.5 | 39.3 to 106.5 | 53.6 to 106.5 |
+| tilt at most, deg | 3.7 | 10.3 | 7.4 | 6.4 | 3.2 | 11.5 |
+| rows with the shell on the floor | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 105 of 284 | 66 of 266 |
+| solved vs keyed: z / pitch | 1.16 mm / 0.55 deg | 0.60 / 0.77 | 0.43 / 0.62 | 0.29 / 0.54 | 3.05 / 1.52 | 2.27 / 0.25 |
+| hard joint margin, rad | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 |
+| soft joint margin, rad | +0.015 | -0.001 (RR knee at 0.44 s) | +0.004 | +0.015 | -0.010 (RF wrist at 1.76 s) | +0.015 |
+| fastest joint, rad/s (corner 30.7) | 11.9 | 16.5 | 8.2 | 12.2 | 12.3 | 10.6 |
+| lowest foot pad, mm | -0.02 | -0.02 | -0.02 | -0.02 | -0.02 | -0.02 |
+| lowest other claw hull, mm | +1.52 | +0.08 | +0.33 | +1.92 | -0.34 | +2.35 |
+| lowest body hull, mm | +6.93 | +6.34 | +6.28 | +6.69 | 0.00 (resting) | -0.28 (resting) |
+| claw to chassis, mm | 23.6 | 25.7 | 25.4 | 9.9 | 18.7 | 26.5 |
+| claw to claw, mm | over 50 | 6.3 | 23.2 | 41.1 | 14.1 | over 50 |
+| leg to leg, mm | 19.8 | 17.7 | 17.4 | 21.9 | 20.9 | 22.8 |
+| least torque, peak N*m (plateau 1.7464) | 0.751 | 0.837 | 0.577 | 0.823 | 0.706 | 0.754 |
+| winding at most (1.0 = derate to 1.2 N*m) | 0.09 | 0.08 | 0.10 | 0.06 | 0.15 | 0.18 |
+| base residual at most, N (limit 0.5) | 0.000 | 0.450 | 0.000 | 0.488 | 0.000 | 0.000 |
+
+The hard margin is the grippers' everywhere: they home 0.1 rad inside their stop. The
+two soft-limit excursions cost 0.01 and 0.1 a step under the joint-limit term, for a few
+rows each; the env_cfgs say so beside the weight. The solved pose differs most where
+the shell rests, because the resting-floor solve reads the feet at their sites and the
+keyed pose stands each foot on the lowest point of its rounded pad. Leg-to-leg counts
+every hull pair on different legs, the claws' shoulders against the walking legs' hips
+included; claw-to-chassis counts each claw beyond its upper arm, which is the part that
+can be driven into the shell (the model does not collide a leg with the chassis at all,
+so physics would not see it).
+
+### The physics control
+
+The clip played in MuJoCo on stiff position servos (kp 400, kd 1, 1 ms, the training
+collision model, base free), compared with the clip's base pose. On the MJCF's own stiff
+contacts, which leave the motion's own errors:
+
+| | swipe | slam | cast | flinch | powerdown | sit |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| height error, median / max, mm | 0.11 / 0.17 | 0.08 / 1.51 | 0.08 / 0.33 | 0.09 / 0.25 | 0.09 / 0.66 | 0.10 / 0.28 |
+| tilt error, median / max, deg | 0.04 / 0.08 | 0.02 / 1.74 | 0.03 / 0.18 | 0.01 / 0.15 | 0.02 / 0.38 | 0.02 / 0.10 |
+| foot and claw floor depth, median / max, mm | 0.01 / 0.16 | 0.01 / 1.95 | 0.01 / 0.17 | 0.01 / 0.31 | 0.01 / 0.36 | 0.01 / 0.16 |
+| rows with leg-on-leg contact | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| stiff-servo torque peak, N*m | 4.60 | 9.09 | 8.45 | 6.09 | 6.96 | 5.54 |
+
+Standing at `HOME` the same run reads 0.07 mm of height error and 0.01 mm of depth. On
+the training contacts, whose silicone compliance sinks the feet 3.39 mm standing at
+`HOME` (2.82 mm of base height), and deeper on four feet than on six:
+
+| | swipe | slam | cast | flinch | powerdown | sit |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| height error, median / max, mm | 3.14 / 3.30 | 3.17 / 6.59 | 5.58 / 6.50 | 2.82 / 3.35 | 2.77 / 5.27 | 2.82 / 5.40 |
+| tilt error, median / max, deg | 0.16 / 0.57 | 0.42 / 10.19 | 2.19 / 5.73 | 0.25 / 0.76 | 0.23 / 0.99 | 0.27 / 1.02 |
+| foot and claw floor depth, median / max, mm | 3.58 / 4.61 | 3.97 / 9.11 | 6.95 / 8.03 | 3.39 / 4.30 | 3.27 / 7.23 | 3.39 / 7.17 |
+
+The stiff-servo torque is an upper bound, not a requirement: six feet closed on the floor
+through kp 400 push against each other. The least torque in the first table is what a
+controller needs. The slam's 10 degrees on the training contacts is the soft floor:
+held still in the same pose (both claws up, body 40 mm back) it tilts 2.2 to 2.7 degrees
+from compression alone, and the claws, which start 3.4 mm deep in it, let go with a
+6.7 N*m spike at 0.40 s, after which 23 rows between 0.50 and 0.94 s have two feet or
+fewer on the floor; on stiff contacts the same clip stays within 1.74 degrees.
+A policy is trained on, and has to balance on, the soft one.
+
+### The speed scans
+
+The fastest pace at which the base can still be balanced on every row, by the same
+inverse dynamics, the move's length in brackets:
+
+| move | pace (base residual at most) |
+|---|---|
+| swipe | 0.84 s (0.56 N), 0.90 s (0.68 N), **0.94 s (0.00 N)**, 1.04 s (0.00 N) |
+| flinch | 0.37 s (4.45 N), 0.41 s (2.23 N), 0.44 s (1.14 N), **0.46 s (0.49 N)** |
+| slam | 1.44 s (0.90 N), 1.62 s (0.67 N), **1.80 s (0.45 N)** |
+
+The least torque at the fastest of these is still under 1.2 N*m for all three (swipe
+0.91, flinch 0.99, slam 1.19 N*m). The game's `hit_react` lasted 0.5 s; the flinch is
+0.46 s.
+
+## Training
+
+The commands as first proposed, before any run (what was run is under "Trained"). On a CUDA
+machine, as the gestures shipped (1000 iterations) and the dances (2000):
+
+```bash
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.skill_swipe     --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 1000
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.skill_flinch    --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 1000
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.skill_slam      --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 1500
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.skill_cast      --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 1500
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.skill_powerdown --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 2000
+python scripts/train.py --task jumper.skill_sit       --backend warp --device cuda:0 --num_envs 4096 --headless --max-iterations 2000
+```
+
+The budgets are suggestions, not measurements. The swipe and the flinch are short clips of
+one claw, as close to the gestures as anything here. The slam and the cast lift both
+claws with the body back, where balance is narrowest. The power-down and the sit step
+every foot, put the shell down and take it off again, which is the most contact change of
+any clip in the repository, so they get the dances' budget. `Episode_Reward/motion_joint_pos`
+and `Episode_Termination/ee_body_pos` (the support feet) are the ones to watch for those
+two.
+
+Then export the checkpoint the run ends on, with no performance video (there is no music):
+
+```bash
+python scripts/export.py --task jumper.skill_swipe --checkpoint logs/jumper/jumper.skill_swipe/<run>/model_999.pt --no-video
+```
+
+### Checked here
+
+Each task trained for five iterations on `native:cpu` with 64 environments (Apple M4
+Pro), from a build that converts its clip through `motion.py`'s checks: joint limits,
+support-foot coplanarity and height, and the speed between control steps. All six built,
+stepped, logged finite rewards and saved `model_4.pt`, at 0.52 to 0.60 s an iteration:
+
